.TH STIME 2
.SH NAME
stime \- set time
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <sys/types.h>
.PP
.B int stime(time_t t);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Stime
sets the system's idea of the time and date.
.I T
is measured in seconds from 0000 GMT Jan 1, 1970.
Only the super-user may use this call.
.PP
.B Note:
v7's page took a POINTER here, and so did this one and the prototype in
lib/libc/sys/syscalls.tbl until
.IR date (1)
became the first caller there had ever been.  It is a VALUE on this machine, for
the reason the notes below give: the gate reads one word out of the argument list
and the generated stub dereferences nothing.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
date(1), time(2), ctime(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Zero is returned if the time was set;
\-1 if user is not the super-user.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B time_t is one word \- 41 bits of seconds, so there is no 2038 problem \- and
.B the gate shed an argument because of it.
v7's
.B time_t
was two PDP-11 words and its gate took both.  See
.IR lseek (2),
which collapsed the same way, and
.IR intro (2)
for why the argument count is a contract between three places and not one.
.SH ASSEMBLER
.B Note:
v7's page gave the PDP-11 form here \- a decimal call number and a
`\fBsys\fP \fIname\fP; \fIarg\fP ...' line.  Neither is how a call is made on this
machine.  The gate is one extracode \-
.B $77 SYS_stime
\- and the C calling convention has already put the arguments where it reads them:
1..n\-1 below r15, the last in the accumulator, the result in the accumulator, the
error number in r14.
.PP
.B The numbering itself is v7's, unchanged.
The macro is defined in <sys/syscall.h>, which is the only place a syscall number
appears on the user side.  See
.IR intro (2)
for the whole contract.
